(a) No cemetery, burial ground, human remains, or burial object shall be knowingly disturbed (see O.C.G.A. § 6-72-4). When property containing a cemetery or burial ground, is developed, the following is required:
(1) Evidence of ownership of the land on which the cemetery or burial ground is located in the form of a legal opinion based upon a title search;
(2) A report prepared by an archeologist stating the number of graves believed to be present and their locations, as can be determined from the use of minimally invasive investigation techniques that do not require a state permit, including remote sensing methods and the use of metal probes;
(3) A survey prepared by a registered surveyor showing the location and boundary in bearings and distances of the cemetery or burial ground based on the aforementioned archeologist's report. The boundary of the cemetery or burial ground shall be staked and offset a minimum of five feet from the location of any grave and the acreage of the cemetery or burial ground shall be provided;
(4) A plan prepared by a genealogist for identifying and notifying the descendants of those buried or believed to be buried in such cemetery. The plan shall be submitted to the planning and zoning department for review and upon approval thereof, the applicant shall implement the plan for notification. The notification shall include a summary of rights under O.C.G.A. § 36-72 and the contact information for the planning and zoning department.
(b) If those buried or believed to be buried are of aboriginal or American Indian descent, the genealogist, in preparing the notification plan, shall consult with the Council of American Indian Concerns and shall include in the notification plan not only any known descendants of those presumed buried but also any American Indian tribes as defined in O.C.G.A. § 44-12-260 that are culturally affiliated; and a proposal for mitigation or avoidance of the effects of the planned activity on the cemetery or burial ground. If the proposal includes relocation of any human remains or burial objects, the proposal shall specify the method of disinterment, the location and method of disposition of the remains, the approximate cost of the process and the approximate number of graves affected.
(Code 1992, § 12-314; Ord. No. 2010-07, § 2, 6-24-2010)

